== Problem ==

Rectangle <imath>DEFA</imath> below is a <imath>3 \times 4</imath> rectangle with <imath>DC=CB=BA=1</imath>. The area of the "bat wings" (shaded area) is

<asy>
draw((0,0)--(3,0)--(3,4)--(0,4)--(0,0)--(2,4)--(3,0));
draw((3,0)--(1,4)--(0,0));
fill((0,0)--(1,4)--(1.5,3)--cycle, black);
fill((3,0)--(2,4)--(1.5,3)--cycle, black);
label("$A$",(3.05,4.2));
label("$B$",(2,4.2));
label("$C$",(1,4.2));
label("$D$",(0,4.2));
label("$E$", (0,-0.2));
label("$F$", (3,-0.2));
label("$1$", (0.5, 4), N);
label("$1$", (1.5, 4), N);
label("$1$", (2.5, 4), N);
label("$4$", (3.2, 2), E);
</asy>

<imath>\textbf{(A) }2\qquad\textbf{(B) }2 \frac{1}{2}\qquad\textbf{(C) }3\qquad\textbf{(D) }3 \frac{1}{2}\qquad \textbf{(E) }4</imath>

==Solution 1==
The area of trapezoid <imath>CBFE</imath> is <imath>\frac{1+3}2\cdot 4=8</imath>. Next, we find the height of each triangle to calculate their area. The two non-colored isosceles triangles are similar, and are in a <imath>3:1</imath> ratio by AA similarity (alternate interior and vertical angles) so the height of the larger is <imath>3,</imath> while the height of the smaller one is <imath>1.</imath> Thus, their areas are <imath>\frac12</imath> and <imath>\frac92</imath>. Subtracting these areas from the trapezoid, we get <imath>8-\frac12-\frac92 =\boxed3</imath>. Therefore, the answer to this problem is <imath>\boxed{\textbf{(C) }3}</imath>.

